15 0 obj
<< /A 44 0 R /Alt (Diagram illustrating a process flow: inputs labeled f\(i,j\) and P3, P4, P5 feed into a stack, followed by a 3D convolution block. The output labeled G\(x,y\) passes through blocks labeled BN and SiLU in sequence. Arrows indicate flow between components.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 34 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 45 0 R /Alt (Diagram showing three different neural network modules: SPPF with Conv, MaxPool2d, and Concat operations; C3k2 with c3k=False using Conv, Split, Bottleneck, and Concat operations; and C3k2 with c3k=True using Conv, Split, C3k, and Concat operations.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 34 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 46 0 R /Alt (Diagram of YOLOv11 Detect architecture with two paths. The top path includes Conv, Conv, Conv2d layers leading to a blue rectangle labeled \215Clou.\216 The bottom path includes DWConv, Conv k=1, DWConv, Conv2d layers, ending with a pink rectangle labeled \215CLSLoss.") /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 47 0 R /Alt (Diagram illustrating two components, C2PSA and PSABlock. On the left, C2PSA shows Conv layers, PSA blocks, concatenation, and output flow. The right depicts PSABlock with an Attention layer, addition operators, and Conv layers, indicating processing flow and connections in a neural network structure.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 48 0 R /Alt (Diagram of a neural network architecture for object detection, titled \215Backbone, Neck, and Head.\216 It begins with an input image of an underwater scene with a diver and corals. The backbone section contains layers labeled \215RepViLayer1\216 to \215RepViLayer5\216 leading to \215C2PSA\216 and \215SPPF\216. The neck section includes \215Conv\216, \215TFE\216, \215Concat\216, \215SSFF\216, \215CPAM\216, and \215C3k2\216 modules. The head section involves \215Detect\216 units. The output shows an annotated version of the input image with boxes identifying and labeling objects like fish and corals with confidence scores.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 36 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 49 0 R /Alt (Flowchart illustrating a processing architecture with three stages labeled Large, Medium, and Small. Each stage includes components for convolution, downsampling, or upsampling. Outputs are merged through a concate step, resulting in a combined output.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 36 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
21 0 obj
<< /A 50 0 R /Alt (Diagram of a neural network architecture split into two parts: channel attention networks and position attention networks. The channel section involves an average pooling layer, Conv1D, sigmoid function, and multiplication operation. The position section involves addition, average pooling, concatenation, ConvBNSiLU, splitting, sigmoid functions, and multiple multiplication operations, concluding with an output.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 37 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
22 0 obj
<< /A 51 0 R /Alt (Figure 8 illustrates the channel\205position attention module. The input is a feature map with dimensions \217batch size × 3 × height × width\220. After successive convolution and pooling operations, the feature map is transformed through channels C1, C2, C3, C4, and finally C5. A fully connected layer follows the pooling stage to generate the final attention weights.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 38 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
23 0 obj
<< /A 52 0 R /Alt (Images display underwater scenes with various marine life such as echinus, starfish, and holothurian, each labeled with detection scores. The images are divided into five columns representing different models: YOLOv5n, YOLOv8n, YOLOXs, YOLOv11n, and UOD-YOLO \(Ours\). Each model\220s effectiveness in identifying marine creatures is shown by the bounding boxes and confidence scores. The backgrounds include rocks and seaweed, illustrating varied marine environments.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 41 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
